  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "The cubic polynomial $P(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1}=-5,$ ${x}_{2}=-4,$ and ${x}_{3}=-3,$ and the coefficient of the cubic term is $-1.$ Find $P(x).$",
  "worked_solution": "Since our cubic polynomial has roots ${x}_{1}=-5,$ ${x}_{1}=-4,$ and ${x}_{3}=-3,$ and the coefficient of the cubic term is $a=-1,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2})(x-{x}_{3}) & =-1(x-(-5))(x-(-4))(x-(-3)) \\\\  & =-(x+5)(x+4)(x+3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(x+5)(x+4)(x+3) & =-({x}^{2}+9x+20)(x+3) \\\\  & =-({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+9{x}^{2}+27x+20x+60) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}-47x-60.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=-{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}-47x-60.$",
